From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 21:03:45 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 17/17] layout: gradients and clip-path

The two biggest remaining holes, and the two that mattered most to real
content: gradients appear in 39 of the Copse app's own files and
clip-path in 19. Gradients were a regression against the rasterization
path -- it renders them and the native path rendered nothing -- and
clip-path was worse than missing, painting content unclipped, which
looks plausible.

Paint servers are collected in one scan of the <svg> subtree at tree
build. Layout has no document-wide id lookup, which is the same gap that
stops <use> working, but a paint server is essentially always defined
inside the <svg> that references it. A reference that escapes resolves
to nothing and the shape goes unpainted, which is visible, rather than
being painted black.

Supported: linear and radial gradients, objectBoundingBox and
userSpaceOnUse units, gradientTransform, spreadMethod, focal points,
gradients on strokes, href/xlink:href stop inheritance with a cycle
guard, and percent-encoded fragment references. clipPath in both unit
systems, on shapes and on groups.

Three things had to be fixed that were not obvious from reading:

stop-color and stop-opacity are gecko-gated in stylo, like
pointer-events before them, so a <stop> had no colour at all. Ungated in
stylo-0003.

clip-path is a presentation attribute Servo does not map, so
clip-path="url(#id)" never reached the cascade and every clip silently
did nothing. Mapped alongside the paint properties. clip-rule is also
gecko-gated and is left alone, so clips use the non-zero rule.

An unresolvable clip-path reference means *ignore the property*, not
*hide the element*. I implemented it backwards first, on the strength of
SVG 1.1's error handling. WPT's
css-masking/clip-path/reference-nonexisting-existing-local.html settles
it: the reference rendering is the unclipped element. Getting this wrong
silently hides content, so it is worth the comment it now carries.

Validated against the rasterization path, which renders both features
correctly: gradients differ by 0.024% of pixels across six variants
(objectBoundingBox, userSpaceOnUse, radial, gradientTransform, on a
circle, on a stroke) and clipping by 0.020% across five, all of it edge
antialiasing between vello and resvg. svg/ WPT: +199 subtests against
the pref-off baseline, zero regressions -- including two tests that
regressed mid-way and prompted the href and percent-decoding work.
